# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Nothing here is a real secret — the local values match
# docker-compose.yml, and the hosted ones are placeholders.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#   docker compose up -d      # local Postgres (needs Docker Desktop)
#   npm run db:migrate        # apply drizzle/*.sql (schema changes: db:generate, then this)
#
# Hosted services: Neon (Postgres) and Cloudflare R2 (files), per docs/02 (ADR-7), plus Resend for
# mail. Auth is Better Auth, in-process (ADR-10), and needs only a secret.

# ── Database ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local development: matches the user/password/db in docker-compose.yml.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://eve:eve@localhost:5432/eve

# Neon (production). Dashboard → Connection Details.
# Use the POOLED string — the host with `-pooler` in it — from the app: a serverless function opens
# a connection per invocation and will exhaust a direct pool. Migrations want the DIRECT host,
# because they need a real session.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://<user>:<password>@ep-<id>-pooler.<region>.aws.neon.tech/eve?sslmode=require
# DIRECT_URL=postgresql://<user>:<password>@ep-<id>.<region>.aws.neon.tech/eve?sslmode=require
#
# DIRECT_URL is WIRED: drizzle.config.ts prefers it and falls back to DATABASE_URL, so migrations
# take the direct host while the app takes the pooled one. Locally there is neither a pooler nor a
# DIRECT_URL, so everything uses DATABASE_URL and there is nothing to set.

# ── Cloudflare R2 — file storage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Gallery photos, product images, venue plan underlays. S3-compatible: the browser PUTs straight
# into the bucket with a presigned URL, so these credentials stay server-only and none of them may
# become a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable.
#
# WIRED, and off until these are set. lib/files/driver.ts uses R2 when ALL FIVE are present and a
# folder on disk otherwise (.uploads/, served by app/api/files) — so uploading works today and the
# switch is these five lines, with nothing above the driver changing. All five or none: a
# half-configured bucket signs uploads that land somewhere and serves reads from a domain that does
# not, which is a gallery of broken images that each cost a storage bill.
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=eve-media
#
# Reads are served by a custom domain on the bucket, never through the Node process — which is the
# whole reason photographs are cheap here: egress out of R2 is free.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_R2_PUBLIC_URL=https://media.eve.studio
#
# ⚠ A SIXTH, OPTIONAL VARIABLE, AND THE ONE THAT WILL WASTE YOUR DAY IF THE BUCKET NEEDS IT.
# A bucket created under a data-location JURISDICTION (Cloudflare offers 'eu' and 'fedramp') lives
# on its own S3 hostname — <account>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com — and is NOT reachable on the
# default one. R2 does not tell you that: it answers a perfectly-signed request sent to the wrong
# host with a bare 403 AccessDenied, which is exactly what it returns for a revoked token or a
# bucket you have no rights to. Leave it unset for an ordinary bucket. If every operation including
# GET is denied, the endpoint is wrong — no permission preset denies reads to a token with access.
# Check the bucket's Location on its dashboard page.
# R2_JURISDICTION=eu
#
# ⚠ TWO THINGS TO SET IN CLOUDFLARE, NOT HERE, or the first upload fails in a way this file cannot
# explain:
#   1. CORS on the bucket — the browser PUTs to it cross-origin. Allow PUT from the app's own
#      origin, with `Content-Type` among the allowed headers.
#   2. Public read on the custom domain, so an <img> can load a photo with no credentials. That is
#      the same exposure the local route has: keys are unguessable uuids and nothing else.
# The signing itself (lib/files/sigv4.ts) is checked against published crypto vectors but has never
# been answered by a real bucket. If the first PUT returns 403, log the canonical request and
# compare it against the one R2 echoes back in the error body — that is where the difference is.

# ── Auth — Better Auth (lib/auth/auth.ts) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign-in, sessions, email verification, password reset and Google sign-in run INSIDE the app on
# the same Postgres (docs/02, ADR-10) — there is no auth service to sign up for. Roles and tenancy
# stay in our own tables; Better Auth answers "who", nothing else.
#
# Signs the session cookie. Without it a database dump would be enough to forge a login, so it lives
# with the deployment and never in the database. Better Auth refuses to start in production without
# it. Generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# ⚠ Changing it signs everybody out (every cookie stops verifying). Nothing else is lost.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
#
# The app's own address (APP_URL, below under Email) is also the auth base URL: origins are checked
# against it and callbacks are built on it. Unset, it is http://localhost:3000.

# ── Google Calendar — diary sync (lib/google/) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two directions: the studio's diary is written into a calendar Eve CREATES inside each designer's
# Google account, and that designer's own calendars come back as read-only "busy" blocks on the
# dashboard. Each studio member connects their own account, in Settings → יומן Google.
#
# WIRED, and off until ALL FOUR are set — lib/google/config.ts returns null otherwise and the
# settings section says so instead of showing a button that cannot work. All four or none: a client
# id without its secret sends the designer through a Google consent screen and then fails on the
# token exchange, which is the worst possible place to fail.
#
# Setting it up, once, per deployment:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → new project → APIs & Services → enable "Google Calendar API".
#   2. OAuth consent screen: External, add the two calendar scopes below plus userinfo.email.
#      ⚠ Those calendar scopes are SENSITIVE. Until Google verifies the app you are capped at ~100
#        test users, added by hand on that screen. Verification takes weeks — start it before you
#        need it, not when the first outside studio signs up.
#   3. Credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application. Authorised redirect URI must match
#      GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI below EXACTLY: scheme, host, port, path, no trailing slash.
#
# Scopes requested (lib/google/config.ts, do not widen without reading the note there):
#   calendar.app.created — manage ONLY the calendar Eve creates. Never touches the primary calendar.
#   calendar.readonly    — read the designer's calendars, for the busy blocks.
#   userinfo.email       — name the connected account in the settings screen.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<id>.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# ⚠ THE ONE VARIABLE THAT IS CORRECT HERE AND WRONG IN PRODUCTION BY DEFAULT. On the deployment it
#   must be https://<your-domain>/api/google/callback, registered on the Cloud project verbatim.
#   Left at the localhost value on a hosted deployment, Google takes the designer through consent
#   and then redirects their browser to their OWN machine — consent granted, code handed to whatever
#   is listening on their loopback, nothing connected, and no trace of it on the server.
#   lib/google/config.ts refuses to start the flow when it detects this, so the failure is a line in
#   settings rather than a dead page; the check does not remove the need to set the variable.
#   Preview deployments cannot do OAuth at all — each has its own hostname, and Google only accepts
#   URIs registered in advance. The settings screen says so rather than offering a button.
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/google/callback
#
# "המשך עם Google" on the sign-in screen is Better Auth's Google provider (lib/auth/auth.ts). It rides
# on the same client id and secret, sends Google this SAME redirect URI, and app/api/google/callback
# hands the sign-in on to Better Auth — nothing more to register on the Cloud project. It asks only
# for `openid email`, and it never creates an account: it signs in the one whose address matches.
#
# Encrypts the stored refresh tokens at rest (AES-256-GCM, lib/google/secret-box.ts). 32 bytes,
# base64 — generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# ⚠ Changing it invalidates every stored connection and each designer must reconnect. It is NOT
#   derived from anything else, so it must be set explicitly and kept with the deployment, not in
#   the database — a dump that contained both the tokens and their key would protect nothing.
# GOOGLE_TOKEN_KEY=

# ── Email — Resend (lib/email/) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Invitations, password resets and email verification. WIRED, and off until ALL THREE are set —
# lib/email/config.ts returns null otherwise, sendEmail() answers "email off" (and prints the message
# to the console in development), and the team screen hides its "שליחה במייל" button. The link to
# copy and the WhatsApp button work either way.
#
# Setting it up, once per deployment:
#   1. resend.com → add and VERIFY the sending domain (DNS records on the domain; until they verify,
#      Resend only delivers to the account's own address). Free tier: 3,000 messages a month.
#   2. API Keys → create a key with "Sending access" only.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# A bare address on the verified domain. The display name is set per message (the studio's name for
# an invitation, "Eve" for account mail), so do NOT write "Eve <...>" here — it is refused.
# EMAIL_FROM=noreply@eve.studio
# ⚠ The address the app answers on: every link in a message is built from it. Configured, never taken
#   from the request — a reset link built from the incoming Host header lets an attacker mail the
#   victim a genuine message whose link delivers the token to the attacker ("reset poisoning").
#   Locally: http://localhost:3000. In production: the real domain, https.
# APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
